{
  "Answer": {
    "Title": "Parallelism is 2, but split output was ignored",
    "Explanation": [
      "The config sets `parallelism: 2` on the `test` job, and the log confirms CIRCLE_NODE_TOTAL=2 with two containers (index 0 and 1). The command does call `circleci tests glob \"src/test_*.sh\" | circleci tests split --split-by=name`, and the split itself worked correctly — node 0's split list is `src/test_goodbye.sh` and node 1's is `src/test_hello.sh`. However, the result is only written to /tmp/split.txt and merely echoed; the actual execution loop iterates over the raw glob `for f in src/test_*.sh`, which expands to all test files on every node. So both nodes ran both tests (\"Running src/test_goodbye.sh\" and \"Running src/test_hello.sh\" on node 0 and node 1), meaning the suite was never effectively split — each container executed the full suite, doing duplicate work and gaining no speedup. The job's own guard then compared RAN=2 against EXPECTED=1 and exited 1 on both nodes, turning the duplication into a red build. The fix, without touching the workflow, is to actually consume the split output in the test run — i.e., feed the per-node file list from `circleci tests split` into the loop (e.g., `while read -r f; do bash \"$f\"; done \u003c/tmp/split.txt` or `bash $(cat /tmp/split.txt)`), so each node only runs its assigned subset. Then RAN will equal EXPECTED on each node and the build goes green with genuine parallel execution. Increasing parallelism, splitting into separate workflow jobs, or dropping parallelism would either not fix the duplication or would violate the requirement to run in parallel without modifying the workflow."
    ],
    "ActualAnswer": [
      "{",
      "  \"each_node_ran_full_suite\": true,",
      "  \"fix\": \"use_circleci_tests_split\",",
      "  \"suite_split\": false",
      "}"
    ],
